# quickshell-bar-watchdog.service — this repo's SECOND custom systemd
# --user unit, beside quickshell.service (18-07, QBAR-10, D-18-40).
#
# What this supervises and why it is a second authored unit rather than a
# uwsm scope: it must outlive transient failures the same way the thing
# it watches does — quickshell.service is the FIRST surface in this repo
# whose process death is a visible desktop outage, and this unit exists
# because the outage recorded at WINDOWS.md row 67 does NOT kill that
# process at all. quickshell stays perfectly healthy — same pid,
# NRestarts=0, ActiveState=active, zero QML errors — while a monitor
# removal/re-add destroys the `quickshell-bar` layer surface underneath
# it and the shell never notices. A `uwsm app` scope has no restart
# policy and no independent supervision lifecycle; this unit needs both,
# exactly the same two properties that justified quickshell.service
# itself.
#
# No [Install] section, and therefore no enable step — same reasoning as
# quickshell.service's own header: enabling would write a wants-symlink
# into the user's own systemd tree that never lands in the repo,
# contradicting this project's core value that the whole setup
# reproduces from a clone plus one script. Omitting the stanza makes that
# failure mode structurally impossible — the unit cannot be enabled even
# by accident (`systemctl --user is-enabled` reports `static`).
# autostart.lua starts it explicitly instead, keeping that file the
# single readable inventory of what a session launches.
#
# PartOf=/After=graphical-session.target and Slice=app-graphical.slice,
# for the same reasons quickshell.service gives: PartOf= is what stops
# this unit at session end, which is what stops the restart policy
# retrying forever against a compositor that no longer exists; the slice
# keeps it in the same cgroup parent as everything else the session
# starts.
#
# Explicitly NOT PartOf=/BindsTo=quickshell.service. This unit's entire
# job is to restart quickshell.service — binding its own lifetime to the
# unit it restarts would tear the watchdog down inside its own recovery
# (a PartOf=/BindsTo= relationship stops or restarts the dependent unit
# whenever the target unit stops), and would couple two restart policies
# that must stay independent: quickshell.service recovering from its own
# crashes is unrelated to this unit recovering the bar surface, and each
# must be free to retry/backoff on its own schedule without the other
# interfering.
#
# The exit-0 contract: bar-watchdog.sh's guard paths (no session, no
# socket, clean EOF at session exit) all `exit 0`, and per
# systemd.service(5) a clean exit does NOT restart under
# Restart=on-failure — so a headless or post-session invocation degrades
# to a logged skip and can never enter a restart cycle. Same reasoning,
# same conclusion as quickshell.service's own guard paths.
#
# No ExecReload=: there is no reload verb, and inventing one would create
# a second untested control path no caller asks for.
#
# Logs go to the journal (`journalctl --user -u
# quickshell-bar-watchdog.service`), which is why the script writes to
# stdout and carries no log file or rotation logic of its own — a
# deliberate divergence from quickshell-launch.sh's ~1MiB rotation, which
# exists because that script predates having a unit at all.

[Unit]
Description=Quickshell bar watchdog (monitor-event surface recovery)
PartOf=graphical-session.target
After=graphical-session.target
StartLimitIntervalSec=60
StartLimitBurst=5

[Service]
Type=simple
Slice=app-graphical.slice
ExecStart=%h/.config/hypr/scripts/bar-watchdog.sh
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=2
